  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nExpansive ridging remains dominate over the western/central \nCONUS this Sunday afternoon. Our region is under its \nnortheastern most influence. Over the top of the ridge, a ridge \nriding wave is passing to our north and east. This brings a \nhighly conditional threat for severe thunderstorms this \nafternoon and evening over portions of northwestern MN.\nPersistent heat will continue into this week as ridging\nremains present in some form. \n\n...Heat Continues...\n\nToday is another hot day. Temperatures have climbed into the \nmid/upper 90s in the central and southern Red River Valley, and are \nnot far behind across much of the rest of the FA. Heat \nheadlines are in effect this afternoon, with the hottest\nconditions expected in the central/southern Red River Valley. \nAs we go through the work week, ridging will remain in control \nover the western/central US, although there are some \ndisagreements in its magnitude and exactly how far eastward its \naxis sets up. Therefore, temperatures will remain above average,\nwith afternoon highs in the 90s across much of the FA until at \nleast midweek. Thereafter, depending on the ridges \namplitude/placement, temperatures will either slightly cool into\nthe 80s, or remain elevated into the 90s. It is during this \nlate week period NBM temperature spreads start to increase, \nshowcasing well ensemble model disagreement surrounding the \nridge. \n\n...Severe Storms Today...\n\nA persistent WAA regime has been in place over northwestern MN this \nmorning, allowing for storms to develop earlier today. However,\nlow level flow has been weakening into the early afternoon. \nThere is indication within some model guidance that flow will \nincrease slightly again later today during the evening hours as \na shortwave rounds the ridge. This may be enough forcing to get\na storm or two to develop. If a storm can form, effective shear\nof over 40 knots and ample instability will help organize \nstorms, with supercells capable of severe hail being the primary\nthreat. It will be very difficult to get a storm to form \nfurther south in west central MN, as this area is far from any \nforcing and fighting a stout cap.",
